%TITLE Measuring Cultural Values in Text without a Cultural Baseline: A Frozen Bilingual Lexicon Approach with Four-Fold Robustness Evidence
%SHORTTITLE Measuring Cultural Values without a Cultural Baseline
%ABSTRACTEN The quantitative measurement of cultural values has long relied on sample surveys, which are costly, infrequently updated, and coarse in granularity. The "text as data" paradigm offers an alternative, yet two questions remain open: whether values represented in text converge with survey-measured national values, and which dimensions of textual difference can be robustly replicated. We propose a measurement pipeline of frozen bilingual lexicon, multilingual embedding, within-text normalization, and dimensional contrast scoring, covering Hofstede's six dimensions, the 13 World Values Survey (WVS) facets, Schwartz's 19 refined values, and the 12 Socialist Core Values (2,559 words). We apply it to three matched corpora—46,274 reviews of the same 3,134 films from Douban and Letterboxd; 27,000 month-aligned news articles from People's Daily-family outlets versus Western mainstream media; and 122 annual governing texts—plus eleven diachronic layers (2005–2026) spanning political, news, and review genres. We report four findings. (1) The multilingual embedding space exhibits a cross-lingual baseline bias that invalidates raw cross-lingual comparison; after within-text normalization, measurements are highly stable across three lexicon versions (text-level r = 0.97–1.00), yet only a minority of group contrasts survive version change. (2) East–West textual differences are register-level and tiered in robustness: indulgence-restraint passes lexicon-version, eleven-layer, placebo, and item-anchor checks and is direction-consistent across three independent encoders; masculinity and power distance are corroborated by three independent anchors; identity, long-/short-term orientation, and uncertainty avoidance are method-dependent and are therefore not treated as findings. (3) The three corpora separate 12 Western outlets from 3 Chinese outlets without overlap on indulgence-restraint. (4) Text-derived scores do not converge with Hofstede country scores or WVS means (only identity shows weak agreement, rho = +0.50; 7 of 13 WVS facets agree in direction), indicating that text measures discourse register rather than national attitudes. We conclude that the four-fold robustness framework should be the reporting standard for text-based cultural measurement, and that symmetric measurement without a presumed cultural baseline is both feasible and necessary.
%KEYWORDSEN cultural values; cross-lingual word embeddings; measurement validity; text mining; computational social science; register versus attitude

# Introduction

The quantitative measurement of cultural values has long relied on sample surveys. Instruments such as the World Values Survey (WVS) and Hofstede's dimensional framework provide authoritative cross-national benchmarks, but they are costly, slow to update, and coarse in granularity. The "text as data" paradigm promises a complementary path: newspapers, reviews, and governing documents are produced continuously, at scale, and in the language that social actors actually use (Gentzkow *et al.*, 2019[[1]]; Grimmer *et al.*, 2022[[2]]).

That promise, however, is not self-fulfilling. Scale does not confer validity; large corpora can amplify systematic bias without making it visible (Lazer *et al.*, 2014[[3]]). Automated content analysis introduces instrumental error that, if left uncorrected, contaminates downstream inference (TeBlunthuis *et al.*, 2024[[4]]). And in the cross-lingual setting, the very act of comparing similarity scores across languages presupposes that such scores are commensurable—an assumption that, as we show below, is false.

This paper makes a methodological contribution to text-based cultural measurement, and reports substantive findings only to the extent that they survive a four-fold robustness framework. Our position is deliberately symmetric: we do not treat any culture's value profile as a baseline against which others are evaluated. Textual differences are interpreted as differences in discourse register—how institutions and platforms speak about values—and not as differences in national attitudes or as deficits of any kind.

We ask three questions. RQ1 (measurement): can a frozen bilingual lexicon combined with multilingual embedding reliably measure value salience in Chinese and Western text? RQ2 (robustness): which dimensions of textual difference survive independent changes of lexicon, model, and anchor? RQ3 (construct boundary): do text-derived value scores converge with survey-based national values, and if not, what do they measure?

# Related Work

## Measuring values: surveys, lexicons, embeddings

Cross-cultural value measurement began with Hofstede's dimensional theory (Hofstede, 2001[[6]]) and was extended by Inglehart's two-axis cultural map (Inglehart, 1997[[5]]) and by Schwartz's theory of basic human values (Schwartz & Cieciuch, 2012[[7]]). Each framework carries known limitations: Hofstede's original data are historically distant; survey instruments are expensive and slow.

Text-based alternatives fall into three families. Dictionary approaches, such as LIWC (Pennebaker *et al.*, 2015[[8]]) and the Personal Values Dictionary (Ponizovskiy *et al.*, 2020[[9]]), are transparent and reproducible but context-insensitive. Word-embedding approaches showed that social biases are encoded in vector spaces and can be quantified and even attenuated (Caliskan *et al.*, 2017[[10]]; Bolukbasi *et al.*, 2016[[11]]; Garg *et al.*, 2018[[12]]). Pretrained language models substantially improved semantic representation (Devlin *et al.*, 2019[[13]]). Our method belongs to the embedding family, but differs in two respects: we freeze the lexicon so that every lexical decision is auditable, and we normalize within text so that cross-lingual comparison does not depend on absolute similarity.

## Cross-lingual measurement and its pitfalls

Unsupervised cross-lingual word embedding alignment (Conneau *et al.*, 2018[[14]]) and large-scale multilingual pretraining (Conneau *et al.*, 2020[[15]]) make a shared representation space feasible (Ruder *et al.*, 2019[[16]]). Yet evaluation robustness is itself a research problem: cross-lingual embedding quality varies sharply with language pair and evaluation protocol (Glavaš *et al.*, 2019[[17]]). The implication for cultural measurement is direct—absolute cross-lingual similarity cannot be compared across languages without correction, a point we operationalize in Section 3.

## Cultural alignment in language models

Recent work has examined which values language models express. Studies have probed model knowledge of cultural moral norms (Ramezani & Xu, 2023[[18]]), measured cultural bias and alignment (Tao *et al.*, 2024[[19]]), and shown that closed-style survey evaluation is unstable under trivial perturbations such as reordering response options (Kabir *et al.*, 2025[[20]]). A systematic survey of "culture" in LLM research distinguishes questionnaire-based, narrative-based, and behavioral measurement paths, and argues for alternatives to forced-choice elicitation (Adilazuarda *et al.*, 2024[[21]]; 2025[[22]]). A complementary survey maps cultural awareness in language models across text and non-text modalities (Pawar *et al.*, 2025[[24]]). Recent studies also show that simulated survey responses lack the diversity of real populations (Liu *et al.*, 2025[[25]]) and that writing assistants measurably homogenize prose toward Western stylistic norms while diminishing cultural nuance (Agarwal *et al.*, 2025[[26]]). Underlying much of this work is a deeper critique of measurement itself: psychological instruments and their samples are disproportionately drawn from Western, educated, industrialized populations, so that "human" baselines are in fact parochial (Atari *et al.*, 2023a[[27]]). This literature is relevant here for a methodological reason: it demonstrates that the instrument can dominate the result, which is precisely the concern our robustness framework addresses for text corpora—and it motivates our refusal to adopt any culture's profile as the measurement baseline.

# Method

## The measurement pipeline

Our pipeline answers a single question: how strongly does a text lean toward a given value facet? It has five steps.

**Step 1 (vectorization).** Every text and every lexicon word is mapped into one shared semantic space by a single multilingual embedding model. Using one model for both languages is essential; otherwise Chinese and English occupy spaces that cannot be compared.

**Step 2 (facet location).** For each value facet, the vectors of all its words are averaged to form a centroid (Equation 1).

**Step 3 (proximity).** The cosine similarity between a text vector and each facet centroid measures how close the text is to that facet (Equation 2).

**Step 4 (within-text normalization).** We find that texts in different languages have systematically different similarity baselines in the shared space. Absolute similarity is therefore not comparable across languages. We normalize each text across all facets of a system, so that only the relative salience of facets within the same text is compared (Equation 3).

**Step 5 (dimensional scoring).** For each dimension, the score is the difference between its two poles (Equation 4). A positive value indicates relative emphasis on the first pole.

>FIGBODY path:figs/figB0_pipeline.png
>FIG Figure 1: The measurement pipeline. A frozen bilingual lexicon and a single multilingual encoder produce facet centroids; texts are scored by cosine proximity, normalized within text to remove the cross-lingual baseline, and reduced to per-dimension contrast scores.
>NOTE Source: drawn by the authors.

$$#c(f)=\frac{1}{|L_f|}\sum_{w\in L_f} e(w)$$
$$#sim(t,f)=\frac{e(t)^{T} c(f)}{\left\Vert e(t) \right\Vert \cdot \left\Vert c(f) \right\Vert}$$
$$#s(t,f)=sim(t,f)-\frac{1}{|F|}\sum_{g\in F} sim(t,g)$$
$$#d(t,D)=s(t,D^{+})-s(t,D^{-})$$

where $e(\cdot)$ denotes an embedding vector, $L_f$ the word list of facet $f$, and $F$ the full set of facets in a value system. All texts and lexicon words are embedded with multilingual-e5-large (Wang *et al.*, 2024[[23]]).

## The frozen lexicon

The lexicon follows a principle of official anchoring, controlled expansion, machine screening, and full traceability. Anchor words are taken from the official item wording of VSM2013, WVS-7, and SVS/PVQ-RR (1,454 words). A large language model expands these into 3,192 bilingual candidates across three types (abstract value terms, issue signal terms, and behavioral or attitudinal terms). Four machine screens are then applied: semantic cohesion, facet discrimination, cross-lingual alignment, and corpus frequency. The final frozen version contains 2,559 words (995 English, 1,564 Chinese). Every decision is logged, so that the lexicon can be audited and the sensitivity of results to lexicon specification can be tested directly—which we do.

## Validation framework

We validate along three axes. At the reliability level, we test cross-lingual alignment quality (ground-truth retrieval AUC, parallel-sentence discrimination) and lexicon-version sensitivity (anchors only, anchors plus unscreened expansion, frozen). At the robustness level, we test direction consistency across eleven diachronic layers, three genres (political, news, review), and individual outlets, plus four independent checks (lexicon version, within-culture placebo, cross-model triangulation, questionnaire-item anchoring). At the criterion level, we test convergence between text-derived contrasts and Hofstede country scores and WVS survey means.

The questionnaire-item anchor deserves emphasis, because it connects our pipeline to a growing methodological literature on anchoring text analysis in psychometric instruments rather than derived word lists (Atari *et al.*, 2023b[[28]]). Recent work in communication methodology likewise argues that language models must be validated against the constructs they are claimed to measure, and that instruction design materially changes both validity and bias (Laurer *et al.*, 2024[[29]]). Our four-fold framework is designed to be a concrete instantiation of that principle for cross-lingual cultural measurement.

# Data

We construct three matched corpora, each designed so that content is held constant and only the discourse community varies. Same-film reviews pair Chinese and English reviews of the *same* film, so that film content is fully controlled. Month-aligned news pairs Chinese and Western coverage from the same month, controlling seasonality and issue cycles. Year-paired governing texts pair Chinese and Western annual governing statements from the same year, controlling historical period. Only under such content alignment can measured differences be attributed to how different communities speak about values rather than to what they are speaking about.

>TAB Table 1: Three-layer corpora and matching design
| Layer | Chinese side | Western side | Size | Matching |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| State (governing texts) | Government Work Reports (1990–2026) | King's/Queen's Speech / SOTU / SOTEU | 122 texts | by year |
| Media (recent news) | People's Daily-family (2026-01–09) | FineNews Western media (2025) | 27,000 articles | same month, adjacent year |
| Public (recent reviews) | Douban (2019 scrape) | Letterboxd (2025 scrape) | 3,134 films / 46,274 reviews | same film and year |
| Historical news | THUCNews (2005–2011) | XSum/BBC (2010–2017) | 20,000 + 20,000 | topic-stratified |
| Historical reviews | Douban | IMDB (Maas *et al.*, 2011) | 10,000 + 10,000 | rating-by-length stratified |
| Chinese films | Douban (Chinese audience) | Letterboxd (English audience) | 618 films / 9,744 reviews | same film, content-identity |
>NOTE Notes. The review sets use title-and-year disambiguation to remove remake/TV version collisions; an additional tt-ID-linked set (via Douban's IMDB column and aclImdb/Tag Genome) provides exact same-film pairing without title ambiguity. Political long documents (mean 23,671 characters) use chunk-mean embedding to avoid truncation.

The corpora further include a strictly same-year news replication set (People's Daily 2025 versus FineNews 2025) and eleven diachronic layers spanning 2005–2026 across political, news, and review genres, including a deep historical layer linked by IMDB title IDs.

# Results

## Measurement reliability

Cross-lingual alignment quality is adequate for shared-space measurement. For 2,000 MUSE ground-truth English–Chinese word pairs, mean cosine similarity is 0.899 versus 0.805 for random pairs (separation 0.094, area under the ROC curve AUC = 0.981); Top-1 retrieval accuracy is 51.5% and Top-5 is 79.4%.

A critical finding concerns the baseline. Raw cosine similarity is systematically higher for Chinese text across all 56 facets (by 0.03–0.06), and the excess applies simultaneously to mutually exclusive poles. Absolute similarity therefore reflects language geometry rather than cultural difference. Within-text normalization removes this component.

>TAB Table 2: Cross-lingual alignment validity of multilingual-e5-large (English–Chinese)
| Metric | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Mean cosine of 2,000 MUSE ground-truth word pairs | 0.899 |
| Mean cosine of random word pairs | 0.805 |
| Separation (ground-truth minus random) | 0.094 |
| ROC AUC (ground-truth vs. random) | 0.981 |
| Top-1 / Top-5 retrieval accuracy | 51.5% / 79.4% |
| Parallel-sentence discrimination | 4/4 correct |
>NOTE Notes. Alignment quality supports shared-space measurement; the cross-lingual baseline bias (all facets elevated by 0.03–0.06 for Chinese text) is corrected by within-text normalization.

Lexicon sensitivity shows the same structure. Across three lexicon versions (anchors only; anchors plus unscreened expansion; frozen), text-level scores correlate at r = 0.97–1.00, but group contrasts are much more fragile: only a minority of dimensions retain their sign across versions. This asymmetry—robust individual measurement, fragile group contrasts—is a basic property of text-based cultural measurement and motivates our reporting rule: no sign-based claim enters the paper unless it survives independent changes of lexicon, model, and anchor.

## Register differences between Chinese and Western discourse

Across the three matched corpora, six Hofstede dimensions show a consistent structure (Table 3). Chinese discourse is more restraint-oriented than Western discourse in all three corpora (−15.15 reviews, −21.13 news, −14.74 political texts, all per mille); Western discourse is relatively more indulgence-oriented. Power distance shows a clean genre pattern: positive in reviews (+2.03) and governing texts (+2.04), negative in news (−2.33). Masculinity is positive in all three and strongest in governing texts (+6.37). These are register-level differences: they describe how institutions and platforms speak, not what populations believe.

>TAB Table 3: Six-dimensional contrast scores (Chinese minus Western, ipsative ×1000) across the three matched corpora
| Dimension | Same-film reviews | Month-aligned news | Governing texts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Power distance | +2.03 | −2.33 | +2.04 |
| Identity (collectivism–individualism) | +0.73 | +1.81 | −1.85 |
| Masculinity | +0.75 | +3.72 | +6.37 |
| Uncertainty avoidance | +4.53 | +4.78 | +4.69 |
| Long-/short-term orientation | −9.68 | −9.23 | −7.89 |
| Indulgence-restraint | −15.10 | −21.13 | −14.74 |
>NOTE Notes. Contrast = pole A minus pole B within each dimension; positive values indicate relative emphasis on the first pole by Chinese text. All contrasts are significant in paired tests (film-/month-/year-level); see the four-fold robustness framework for which are retained as findings.

The differences hold at the outlet level. On indulgence-restraint, all 12 Western outlets (Independent, Fox, Telegraph, WaPo, NYT, CNN, Reuters, AP, BBC, USAToday, Guardian, NPR) are positive (+11.0 to +22.8) and all 3 Chinese outlets are negative (−3.4 to −8.5), with zero overlap; long-/short-term orientation separates the outlets completely as well (Figure 3). On diachronic replication across eleven layers spanning 2005–2026, uncertainty avoidance, long-/short-term orientation, and indulgence-restraint are direction-consistent in every layer; power distance follows the genre pattern (positive in review and state layers, negative in recent news layers) (Figure 2).

