## The four-fold robustness framework

We subject every candidate finding to four independent checks, plus two data-processing checks (Figure 4 summarizes the evidence).

**Lexicon-version sensitivity.** Contrasts are recomputed under three lexicon specifications. Only dimensions that keep their sign across versions are eligible.

**Within-culture placebo.** We compare Chinese outlets with one another and Western outlets with one another to establish a noise floor for "two arbitrary outlets." The ratio of cross-cultural difference to within-culture noise is 5.75 for indulgence-restraint and 3.97 for long-/short-term orientation, but only 0.56–1.34 for power distance, identity, masculinity, and uncertainty avoidance. Only indulgence-restraint and long-/short-term orientation are measured with specificity above outlet-level noise.

**Cross-model triangulation.** The pipeline is rerun with two further, architecturally independent multilingual encoders (LaBSE and BGE-M3, in addition to multilingual-e5-large). Indulgence-restraint is direction-consistent across all three encoders on the same-film review and month-aligned news corpora; masculinity is direction-consistent across all three encoders on all three corpora; power distance is consistent on two of three; identity, long-/short-term orientation, and uncertainty avoidance are not model-invariant. On long governing documents, encoder behavior is more sensitive to document-length handling, so we corroborate the political-layer result with the two encoders that use chunk-mean full-text embedding and do not treat it as model-invariant.

**Questionnaire-item anchoring.** The anchor is replaced by the official VSM2013 item statements themselves—the operational definitions—rather than a derived word list. Power distance, indulgence-restraint, and masculinity agree with the lexicon-based result on all three corpora (text-level r = 0.82–0.90); identity agrees on two; long-/short-term orientation and uncertainty avoidance flip entirely (r = 0.24–0.27), indicating that these two dimensions reflect lexical specification rather than the construct.

**Data-processing checks.** Replacing 2,000-character truncation with full-text chunk-mean embedding leaves all six dimensions direction-identical in both the news and review corpora (12 of 12). Reconstructing the news comparison with strictly same-year alignment (Chinese 2025 versus Western 2025) reproduces all six directions (6 of 6). Split-half reliability on the governing texts is high (mean r = 0.883).

Taken together, the evidence is tiered. **Indulgence-restraint is the most robust dimension**—it passes lexicon-version, eleven-layer, placebo, and item-anchor checks, and is consistent across three encoders on the shorter-document corpora—and is reported as the core finding. **Power distance and masculinity** are corroborated by three independent anchors (and masculinity is now consistent across all three encoders on all corpora) and are reported as secondary. **Identity, long-/short-term orientation, and uncertainty avoidance** are method- or model-dependent; we report their instability as a finding about measurement rather than a finding about culture.

>TAB Table 4: Four-fold robustness pass summary per dimension
| Dimension | Lexicon version | 11-layer | Placebo | Cross-model | Item anchor | Verdict |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Indulgence-restraint | pass | pass | pass | pass | pass | core |
| Power distance | pass | partial | fail | partial | pass | secondary |
| Masculinity | fail | pass | fail | pass | pass | secondary |
| Long-/short-term orientation | fail | pass | pass | partial | fail | not reported |
| Identity | fail | fail | fail | fail | partial | not reported |
| Uncertainty avoidance | fail | pass | fail | fail | fail | not reported |
>NOTE Notes. "pass" = direction retained under the check; "partial" = genre- or model-dependent; "fail" = not retained. The cross-model column spans three encoders (multilingual-e5-large, LaBSE, BGE-M3); the item-anchor column uses the official VSM2013 item statements as the anchor.

