## Construct boundary: register, not attitude

If text measured national values, text-derived scores should converge with survey benchmarks. They do not (Figure 5). Across Chinese, US, and UK rankings on Hofstede's six dimensions, only identity shows weak agreement (Spearman's rho = +0.50); indulgence-restraint is exactly opposite (rho = −1.00). Across 13 WVS facets, only 7 agree in direction with survey means—close to chance. The clearest decoupling involves corruption: Chinese news discourse is far less corruption-salient than Western news discourse (−18.6 per mille), whereas WVS data do not show lower corruption concern among Chinese respondents. Salience and attitude are different constructs.

>TAB Table 5: Criterion validity—rank correlation between text-derived contrasts and survey benchmarks (China/US/UK)
| Dimension | Spearman's rho (vs. Hofstede country scores) |
| --- | --- |
| Power distance | −0.50 |
| Identity | +0.50 |
| Masculinity | 0.00 |
| Uncertainty avoidance | −0.50 |
| Long-/short-term orientation | 0.00 |
| Indulgence-restraint | −1.00 |
>NOTE Notes. On WVS-7 (2017–2022), only 7 of 13 facets agree in direction between text salience and survey means—approximately chance. Text-derived salience and survey-measured attitudes are distinct constructs; the text measures discourse register, not national values.

>FIGBODY path:figs/figB2_construct_boundary.png
>FIG Figure 5: Construct boundary. Left: rank correlation between text-derived contrasts and Hofstede country scores across China, the US, and the UK. Right: number of WVS facets whose text-salience direction agrees with survey means. Convergence is at or near chance, indicating that text and survey measure different constructs.
>NOTE Source: drawn by the authors.

We therefore state an explicit construct boundary: **all East–West differences reported here are differences in discourse register, measurable at the level of text. They are not causal or attitudinal claims about national values.** Text characterizes how institutions and platforms speak about values, not how populations hold them.

## What the registers contain

Characterizing the two registers directly clarifies the finding. On the WVS and Schwartz systems, Western news is relatively higher on problem-, norm-, constraint-, and care-oriented facets (corruption, conformity to rules, social safety, benevolent care, universalism), whereas Chinese news is relatively higher on development-, vision-, and positive-experience facets (happiness, technology, hedonism, self-directed thought, post-materialism, tradition). The dominant axis of East–West textual difference is thus not a mirror of national values but a difference in institutional discourse register: the Chinese register is a development-and-ethics register, the Western register a rights-and-fairness register.

The Chinese register, measured on the Socialist Core Values, is itself internally coherent, temporally stable, and shared across layers. Government Work Reports, official media, and public film reviews show profile correlations of 0.966, 0.810, and 0.658 (mean 0.811), and the four official outlets are near-identical. Prosperity ranks first in all three layers and dedication is nearly identical across them; the decade-long public trajectory (2010–2019) and the monthly official trajectory fluctuate by less than 1.5 per mille. Finally, content cultural identity moderates value expression: the same Chinese audience expresses its development-and-ethics register more strongly when reviewing domestic Chinese films than foreign films (identity +1.39 versus +0.73; power distance +2.51 versus +2.04; masculinity +2.41 versus +0.77). This is an association, not a causal claim; it indicates that discourse registers flex with the cultural identity of the content under discussion.

# Discussion

Our contribution is methodological and, we believe, generalizable beyond the particular comparison reported here.

First, we identify and correct a cross-lingual baseline bias that silently affects any study comparing embedding similarity across languages. Within-text normalization is a simple and portable remedy.

Second, we show that a four-fold robustness framework is both feasible and necessary. Individual-level text measurement is stable; group-level contrasts are not. Without independent variation of lexicon, model, and anchor, a substantial share of published group contrasts may reflect the instrument rather than the construct. We recommend the four checks as a reporting standard.

Third, we demonstrate a symmetric measurement stance. Treating one culture's profile as the baseline against which others are scored is not only an ethical problem but a measurement error: it converts register differences into apparent deficits and obscures the internal coherence of each profile. The same logic has been pressed against psychology's own sampling base, where instruments calibrated on Western populations are treated as universal (Atari *et al.*, 2023a[[27]]); our results extend that critique to computational measurement of culture. Our results show that the Chinese value register is internally consistent, temporally stable, and shared across state, media, and public discourse—an object of study in its own right, not a deviation from a presumed norm.

Finally, the construct boundary we document is itself a substantive result. Text corpora are increasingly used as proxies for public opinion. Our finding that text-derived value salience does not converge with survey-measured attitudes cautions against that substitution—and, more constructively, identifies what text corpora are uniquely good for: tracking how institutions and platforms frame values over time and across communities.

# Limitations

Three limitations remain. First, the Western news sample is constrained by robots-protocol availability, so BBC, Reuters, and AP are structurally absent; conclusions are bounded to accessible media text, though outlet-level analysis shows that the 12 Western outlets and 3 Chinese outlets separate without overlap, so local sampling skew cannot explain a separation of this magnitude. Second, the public-layer corpus consists of film reviews, whose value expression is shaped by viewing agendas; extension to social media is desirable. Third, our measurement rests on embedding models; although we triangulate three architecturally independent encoders and a questionnaire-item anchor, further triangulation with additional models and with dictionary- or LLM-based annotation would strengthen the framework. We note, however, that the main threats to validity have been addressed directly: text truncation is shown not to alter conclusions (12 of 12 direction-identical), adjacent-year alignment is corroborated by strict same-year alignment (6 of 6), and same-film version collisions are removed by title-and-year disambiguation plus IMDB-title-ID linkage.

